                    round_name_r2:          `Convene`,
                    round_name_r3:          `Justify the Decision`,
                    round_name_r4:          `Rebuild Legitimacy`,

                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Map the value conflict`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `A leaked ecological review finds your consented, 40%-financed <strong>90 MW Sliabh Bán</strong> project will damage ~14 hectares of culturally significant protected peatland. The stakeholders who were aligned now hold irreconcilable demands: ~1,200 <strong>residents</strong> and a heritage group want it halted or relocated on the trust you promised; the infrastructure-fund <strong>investor</strong> wants its EUR 140M and ESG-flagship returns; an environmental <strong>NGO</strong> demands habitat protection yet warns halting loses ~180,000 t/yr of avoided CO₂; ~220 <strong>jobs</strong> and EUR 18M of local spend hang on it proceeding. Do not rush to a fix. <em>Diagnose this as a clash of legitimate values</em> — and decide who has an affectedness-based claim to a voice, not just power or noise.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Convene the deliberative process`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Design a <strong>legitimate deliberative process</strong>, not a PR exercise. Decide who is convened, on what terms, what information is shared transparently, and the decision rule the process commits to. Schormair’s point is that justification requires you would <em>actually act</em> on the reasons raised. The resident will test you directly: “If we object, will anything change?” A forum with the redesign already locked is <strong>consultation theatre</strong> — and once exposed it destroys more trust than no consultation at all. Choose a process you would genuinely act on, with the transparency to back it.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Justify the contested decision`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Now <strong>make and discursively justify</strong> the decision on Sliabh Bán — knowing it will disappoint someone. Proceed (protect the EUR 140M, leave a live harm), redesign/relocate (a costly compromise, up to EUR 22M, that destroys margin but is defensible), halt (full protection, lose the avoided CO₂ and EUR 18M local spend), or cut a quiet EUR 3M settlement (which confirms bad faith when it leaks). Commit a <strong>remediation</strong> proportionate to the harm your decision actually leaves behind. And justify it <em>discursively</em> — reasons each affected party could reasonably accept — not “the consent permits it,” which is legally true and ethically inert.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Rebuild legitimacy and pitch`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `A stakeholder publicly rejects the decision and a journalist asks whether the process was genuine. Preserve the <strong>legitimacy of the process</strong> even where the outcome is contested — show how reasons changed the decision and concede honestly what still disappoints some. Then make the 3-minute case to the investment committee <em>and</em> a community representative <strong>at the same time</strong>: one honest story, demonstrating that a defensible deliberative process protected both the returns and the trust better than a quiet deal would have. Chasing full consensus now only runs out the financing window — legitimacy comes from the process, not unanimity.`,

                    r1_diag_label:          `How do you diagnose the dispute?`,
                    r1_voice_label:         `Who has a legitimate claim to a voice at the table? (select the principle)`,
                    r1_separate_label:      `How clearly do you separate irreducible value clashes from negotiable interest differences? (%)`,
                    r1_separate_readout:    `Clarity of the value-conflict map:`,
                    diag_values:            `A clash of legitimate values — community trust & heritage, climate benefit, fiduciary duty, livelihoods and ecology genuinely conflict; no option satisfies all`,
                    diag_pr:                `A reputation problem — fundamentally a communications crisis to be managed with the right messaging`,
                    diag_loudest:           `A negotiation with the loudest party — find what the local committee wants and make them go quiet`,
                    diag_legal:             `A legal exposure — we hold valid consent; this is about defending our right to build`,
                    claim_affected:         `Everyone genuinely affected — residents & heritage group, NGO, employees/contractors, regulator and investor — affectedness, not just power or noise`,
                    claim_power:            `Only those with power over us — the investor and the regulator; the rest we inform`,
                    claim_loud:             `Only the loudest — the local committee and the press; quiet them and the problem fades`,
                    claim_internal:         `Keep it internal — board and legal decide; external voices slow us past the financing window`,

                    r2_process_label:       `Design the deliberative process.`,
                    r2_transparency_label:  `Information shared transparently with participants (%)`,
                    r2_transparency_readout: `Transparency:`,
                    r2_pct:                 `%`,
                    r2_transparency_hint:   `Sharing the ecological review, the financials and the legal exposure builds trust — but you cannot un-share what you reveal, and concealment, once exposed, destroys more than openness ever risked.`,
                    r2_rule_label:          `The decision rule the process commits to.`,
                    proc_deliberative:      `A genuine deliberation — all affected parties convened, full information shared, ground rules agreed, and an outcome you would actually act on`,
                    proc_theatre:           `A consultation forum with the redesign already locked — look consultative, keep control of the outcome`,
                    proc_bilateral:         `Quiet bilateral talks with the investor and the committee separately — never put them in the same room`,
                    proc_none:              `No process — decide internally and announce; consultation only slows the 45-day window`,
                    rule_act:               `We will act on the reasons raised — if affected parties show a redesign is justified, the decision changes`,
                    rule_advisory:          `Advisory only — we listen, then management decides whatever it intended anyway`,
                    rule_consensus:         `Nothing proceeds without unanimous agreement from every party`,

                    r3_decision_label:      `The contested decision on Sliabh Bán.`,
                    r3_remediation_label:   `Harm remediation committed to the peatland & community (EUR M, 0–22)`,
                    r3_remediation_hint:    `Proportionate to the chosen path: full relocation runs to EUR 22M and destroys the project margin; EUR 0 maximises return but leaves a live, visible harm. Remediation should match the harm your decision actually leaves behind.`,
                    r3_justify_label:       `How do you justify the decision to the stakeholders it disappoints?`,
                    dec_redesign:           `Redesign / relocate turbines away from the peatland — a costly compromise that disappoints the investor but is defensible to all`,
                    dec_proceed:            `Proceed as-is to protect the EUR 140M — treat the community as a comms problem to be managed`,
                    dec_halt:               `Halt the project entirely — protect the peatland fully, accept losing ~180,000 t/yr of avoided CO₂ and the EUR 18M local spend`,
                    dec_settle:             `Quiet cash settlement — pay the local committee EUR 3M for their silence and press on as-is`,
                    just_discursive:        `Discursively — give reasons each affected party could reasonably accept, naming openly what is being traded off and why`,
                    just_compliance:        `By compliance — “the planning consent permits it”; we are within our legal rights`,
                    just_authority:         `By authority — explain that the board has weighed it and this is the company’s decision to make`,
                    just_silence:           `Minimal — announce the decision and let it speak for itself; do not invite further debate`,

                    r4_response_label:      `A stakeholder publicly rejects the decision and a journalist asks if the process was genuine. Respond.`,
                    r4_pitch_label:         `The 3-minute pitch to the investment committee AND a community representative — at the same time.`,
                    r4_consensus_label:     `Where on Schormair’s continuum did you realistically aim?`,
                    r4_conviction_label:    `Conviction: how firmly do you hold that a legitimate process beats a quiet deal, even when it disappoints someone? (%)`,
                    resp_legitimacy:        `Defend the legitimacy of the process — show how reasons changed the decision, concede the outcome still disappoints some, and stand on the integrity of how it was reached`,
                    resp_spin:              `Restate the “developer communities trust” mission and decline to discuss how the decision was made`,
                    resp_dismiss:           `Dismiss the objector as a vocal minority and point to the valid planning consent`,
                    resp_silence:           `No comment — refuse the enquiry and let the decision stand on its own`,
                    pitch_dual:             `One honest story to both — a defensible deliberative process protected the returns the committee can stand behind AND the trust the community needs; no separate messages`,
                    pitch_split:            `Tell each audience what it wants to hear — returns to the committee, contrition to the community; hope the two versions never meet`,
                    pitch_returns:          `Lead with returns — reassure the committee first; the community representative will follow the money`,
                    cont_compromise:        `Value compromise — or at least an honest “agreement to disagree”; legitimacy comes from the process, not unanimity`,
                    cont_consensus:         `Full consensus only — keep deliberating until every party fully agrees`,
                    cont_dissensus:         `Accept dissensus and move on — note the disagreement and decide regardless`,

                    warn_separate_range:    `The value-conflict-map clarity must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_transparency_range: `Transparency must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_remediation_range: `Remediation must be between EUR 0M and EUR 22M.`,
                    warn_conviction_range:  `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_values:        `<strong>That is the right diagnosis.</strong> This is a clash of <em>legitimate</em> values — community trust and heritage, climate benefit, fiduciary duty, livelihoods, ecology — and no single option satisfies all of them. Naming it that way is what lets you design a process that earns legitimacy rather than papers over the conflict.`,
                    coach_r1_pr:            `<strong>Treating this as a communications crisis is Common Error number three.</strong> The peatland harm is real and the affected community has a legitimate claim — you cannot message your way out of a value conflict. If Communications “manages the narrative” while the substance is decided elsewhere, the gap is exactly what the journalist writes.`,
                    coach_r1_loudest:       `<strong>“Find what the committee wants and make them go quiet” is the road to the quiet cash deal.</strong> Salience is about affectedness, not noise. Resolve this by buying off the loudest party and you have confirmed the worst suspicion about you the moment it leaks.`,
                    coach_r1_legal:         `<strong>“We hold valid consent” is legally true and ethically inert.</strong> Compliance is the floor, not a reason a grieving community can accept. Framing this as defending your right to build skips the entire question of justification.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named it as a clash of legitimate values AND gave a voice to everyone genuinely affected — affectedness, not power or noise. That is the foundation a legitimate process is built on; now design one you would actually act on.`,
                    coach_r1_loud:          `<strong>Giving a voice only to the loudest is how you mistake noise for salience.</strong> The quiet stakeholders — the NGO’s dissenting wing, the contractors, the heritage group — have legitimate claims too. Hear affectedness, not volume.`,
                    coach_r1_internal:      `<strong>Deciding internally and informing the rest is not a process — it is an announcement.</strong> External voices feel like delay, but excluding the affected is precisely what costs you legitimacy. The financing window is a reason to design a fast process, not to skip one.`,
                    coach_r1_lowclarity:    `<strong>Without separating irreducible value clashes from negotiable interests, you cannot see where a compromise exists.</strong> Some conflicts only look irreducible until the process surfaces a redesign everyone can live with. Map them clearly first.`,
                    coach_r2_deliberative:  `<strong>A genuine deliberation is the legitimate choice.</strong> Convening all affected parties with full information and agreed ground rules is what moves people along Schormair’s continuum — toward compromise, or at least an honest agreement to disagree. Now commit to a decision rule you would truly act on.`,
                    coach_r2_theatre:       `<strong>That is consultation theatre — Common Error number two.</strong> You convened a forum but the redesign was locked before they walked in. A resident asks, “If we object, will anything change?” Your honest answer is no. The moment that lands, you have spent trust to look consultative. Justification requires you would actually act.`,
                    coach_r2_bilateral:     `<strong>Separate back-room talks keep you in control and the parties in the dark.</strong> Never putting the investor and the community in the same room means no shared information, no tested reasons — and it looks exactly like the quiet deal you should be avoiding.`,
                    coach_r2_none:          `<strong>No process at all is the fastest route to a judicial review.</strong> Announcing an internal decision on a contested peatland project, with affected parties never heard, is what gets your ecological assessment challenged — and a 12–18 month freeze costs you the returns you were trying to protect.`,
                    coach_r2_opaque:        `<strong>A deliberation without transparency is theatre with extra steps.</strong> If you withhold the ecological review and the financials, participants cannot reason about the real trade-offs — and concealment, once exposed, destroys more trust than openness ever risked.`,
                    coach_r2_integrity:     `<strong>That is procedural integrity.</strong> A genuine deliberation plus a binding decision rule — if affected parties show a redesign is justified, the decision changes — is what makes the process real rather than performed. This is the standard Schormair means by justification.`,
                    coach_r2_advisory:      `<strong>“Advisory only — then management decides what it intended anyway” is theatre.</strong> If the outcome is fixed before the room convenes, you are not deliberating; you are staging consent. The resident’s question — “will anything change?” — has only one honest answer, and it is fatal.`,
                    coach_r2_consensus:     `<strong>Requiring unanimity sounds principled but is a trap.</strong> Consensus was never available between halting and proceeding; insist on it and the 45-day window closes while you deliberate. Schormair’s continuum includes compromise and agreement to disagree as legitimate ends.`,
                    coach_r3_redesign:      `<strong>Redesign is the defensible compromise.</strong> Relocating the turbines away from the peatland disappoints the investor on cost but gives every affected party a reason they could accept. The margin hit is the price of legitimacy — and a process the investor can stand behind protects the rest of the capital.`,
                    coach_r3_proceed:       `<strong>You proceeded as-is and assigned Communications to “manage the narrative.”</strong> The peatland harm is real and the affected community had a legitimate claim you overrode by power. A judicial review just froze you for 14 months — the EUR 140M you protected is gone <em>and</em> so is the trust. Whose values did you refuse to even hear?`,
                    coach_r3_proceedlite:   `<strong>Proceeding as-is still overrides the affected community by power.</strong> The remediation softens the live harm, but the underlying decision answers a value conflict with a fiduciary reflex. Be honest about whose claim you set aside, and why a reason beyond “returns” would justify it.`,
                    coach_r3_halt:          `<strong>Halting protects the peatland fully — but it is not automatically the legitimate answer.</strong> You have just lost ~180,000 t/yr of avoided CO₂ and EUR 18M of local spend; the NGO’s dissenting wing and the contractors have legitimate claims too. Legitimacy is whether you can justify the trade-off, not which side you pick.`,
                    coach_r3_settle:        `<strong>You paid the local committee EUR 3M for their silence and pressed on — Common Error number one.</strong> Two weeks later a member leaks the side-deal. The NGO, the regulator and every <em>other</em> community read it as a bribe, your reputation collapsed, and the value you sold — being the developer communities trust — is gone. You resolved a conflict by confirming the worst suspicion about you.`,
                    coach_r3_underremediate: `<strong>Relocating but committing almost nothing to remediation undercuts your own decision.</strong> A defensible compromise needs the remediation to match the harm and the cost the path implies; a token sum reads as redesign-on-paper. Make the commitment proportionate.`,
                    coach_r3_compliance:    `<strong>“The planning consent permits it” is Common Error number five.</strong> Legally true, ethically inert. Compliance is the floor, not a reason a grieving community can accept. Discursive justification means giving reasons <em>they</em> could reasonably accept — what reason did you give beyond “we’re allowed”?`,
                    coach_r3_authority:     `<strong>“The board has weighed it” asserts authority instead of offering reasons.</strong> Legitimacy does not come from who decided but from whether the decision is justifiable to those it affects. Tell them <em>why</em>, in terms they could accept — not merely that you were entitled to.`,
                    coach_r3_silence:       `<strong>Announcing the decision and refusing further debate forfeits justification entirely.</strong> A contested decision that disappoints people needs reasons offered openly, not a wall. Silence here reads as having no defensible reason — exactly what the journalist will report.`,
                    coach_r3_justgood:      `<strong>Redesign, justified discursively — that is the textbook move.</strong> A costly compromise <em>plus</em> reasons each affected party could reasonably accept, naming openly what is traded off and why, is what earns legitimacy even from those it disappoints. The investor gets a process it can stand behind.`,
                    coach_r4_legitimacy:    `<strong>Defending the legitimacy of the process is the right response.</strong> Show how the reasons raised changed the decision, concede honestly that the outcome still disappoints some, and stand on the integrity of how it was reached. An unpopular decision can be legitimate — that is the whole point.`,
                    coach_r4_spin:          `<strong>Restating the mission and refusing to discuss how the decision was made is spin.</strong> “The developer communities trust” is the claim under question — repeating the slogan instead of evidencing the process is exactly what an exposé needs.`,
                    coach_r4_dismiss:       `<strong>Dismissing the objector as a “vocal minority” and pointing to the consent confirms you never heard them.</strong> Affectedness is not a numbers game, and “we’re within consent” is the legality dodge again. You have handed the journalist the story in one sentence.`,
                    coach_r4_silence:       `<strong>No comment hands the journalist the narrative.</strong> A contested decision under public challenge needs you to stand behind your process — silence reads as guilt, and the exposé runs without your side of it while the investors watch the reputation index fall.`,
                    coach_r4_dual:          `<strong>One honest story to both audiences — that is the proof of the whole approach.</strong> A defensible deliberative process protected the returns the committee can stand behind <em>and</em> the trust the community needs. No separate messages means nothing to expose, and you have shown that legitimacy beat the quiet deal on both counts.`,
                    coach_r4_split:         `<strong>Telling each audience what it wants to hear is the quiet deal in a new costume.</strong> Returns to the committee, contrition to the community, and a prayer the two versions never meet — they will. The moment the messages collide, the bad faith you avoided in Round 3 lands here instead.`,
                    coach_r4_returns:       `<strong>Leading with returns and assuming the community “will follow the money” repeats the original error.</strong> The community representative is in the room because trust is the value at stake; treating them as secondary to the committee is exactly the power-over-affectedness reflex the case is about.`,
                    coach_r4_consensus:     `<strong>You kept deliberating for full agreement and the 45-day financing window closed — Common Error number four.</strong> Consensus was never available between halting and proceeding; the project collapsed by default while you sought it. Compromise and even agreement to disagree are legitimate outcomes — why did you treat unanimity as the only acceptable end?`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
