                    insight_legitimate:     `Legitimate and defensible: you diagnosed a clash of legitimate values, ran a genuine deliberation you would act on, justified a contested redesign with reasons each party could accept, and faced the press with one honest story. The decision disappoints someone — and is still legitimate. That is the lesson.`,
                    insight_settled:        `Bad faith exposed: the quiet EUR 3M settlement bought two weeks of silence, then a leak turned a value conflict into a legitimacy collapse. The cheapest way out was the most expensive — you confirmed the worst suspicion about “the developer communities trust.”`,
                    insight_frozen:         `Judicial review freeze: proceeding as-is while overriding the affected community by power got your ecological assessment challenged, freezing the project for 14 months. The returns you protected are gone and so is the trust — both, for refusing to hear a legitimate claim.`,
                    insight_window:         `Window closed by default: chasing full consensus that was never available let the 45-day financing window lapse, and the project collapsed without a decision. Schormair’s continuum includes compromise and agreement to disagree — unanimity was the wrong target.`,
                    insight_short:          `You did not fully convert the conflict into a legitimate process. The decision, the deliberation and the justification still leave a seam — enough for a disappointed stakeholder or a journalist to question whether the process was real.`,
                    insight_popular_illegit: `Your reputation outran your legitimacy — popular but not fully justifiable. A favourable read today is fragile if the decision could not survive the question “what reason could the disappointed parties accept?” Reputation is what they think; legitimacy is whether you were right to act.`,
                    insight_unpopular_legit: `Your legitimacy outran your reputation — the mark of an unpopular but defensible decision. Some stakeholders are disappointed, yet the process gave reasons they could reasonably accept. That divergence is exactly what the case teaches: legitimacy is not a popularity score.`,
                    insight_theatre:        `Your process was read as theatre at some point — a forum with the outcome pre-decided, or a rule you would not actually act on. Once a resident’s “will anything change?” has the answer “no,” consultation costs more trust than none at all.`,
                    insight_margin:         `The project margin took a heavy hit. A relocation or a halt is legitimate if you can justify the trade-off — but be clear-eyed that legitimacy was not free, and that an investor will only stand behind a process whose reasoning it can defend.`,
                    insight_voice:          `Affected stakeholders were genuinely heard and the decision was justifiable to them — voice and legitimacy moved together. That alignment, not a high reputation number, is what protects both returns and trust over time.`,

                    hint_settled:           `You cut a quiet cash deal. Expect a leak: a back-room settlement that buys silence confirms bad faith the moment it surfaces and collapses the trust you sell.`,
                    hint_theatre:           `Your process is reading as theatre. If the outcome is fixed before the room convenes, a resident’s “will anything change?” has a fatal answer — make the decision rule one you would actually act on.`,
                    hint_frozen:            `A judicial review has frozen the project. Overriding an affected community by power invites a challenge to your ecological assessment — the returns you protected are now lost to the freeze.`,
                    hint_window:            `Chasing full consensus is running out the 45-day financing window. Compromise and an honest agreement to disagree are legitimate outcomes — do not let unanimity become a decision by default.`,
                    hint_legit:             `Legitimacy is dangerously low. Offer reasons the disappointed parties could reasonably accept rather than authority or compliance, and make the deliberation one you would genuinely act on.`,
                    hint_divergence:        `Your reputation is higher than your legitimacy — a popular move that may not be defensible. Ask whether the decision could survive the question “what reason could the disappointed parties accept?”`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — legitimacy and voice both healthy. Hold the line: defend the integrity of the process under challenge and tell one honest story to both audiences.`,

